      "title": "Open Rank Professor-Psychiatry",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States The Department of Psychiatry at the University of Tennessee Health Sciences in Memphis, Tennessee, is seeking full-time non-tenure track Geriatric Psychiatrist. This is an Open Rank faculty position. The position is largely outpatient psychiatry, and the faculty member is expected to work closely with neurology and related services in a Memory Care Clinic setting. The Department of Psychiatry at the University of Tennessee Health Sciences in Memphis, Tennessee, is seeking full-time non-tenure track Geriatric Psychiatrist. This is an Open Rank faculty position. The position is largely outpatient psychiatry, and the faculty member is expected to work closely with neurology and related services in a Memory Care Clinic setting. EDUCATION: MD, DO, or equivalent. LICENSES: Board certification in Psychiatry by the ABPN is required. Completion of an accredited residency or equivalent, as permitted by state law, is required and sub-certifications/training in Geriatric Psychiatry is highly preferred. International Medical Graduates who have completed an ACGME-accredited residency are encouraged to apply. A TN Medical License and DEA License is required at the time of onboarding. Interested candidates should submit a letter of interest and Curriculum Vitae to: Ronald Cowan, MD, PhD, FAPA Chair, Department of Psychiatry, 920 Madison Avenue, Room 262, Memphis, TN 38163 For benefits information, please visit https://www.uthsc.edu/hr/benefits/documents/benefits-preview-packet.pdf THE UNIVERSITY OF TENNESSEE HEALTH SCIENCES The University of Tennessee Health Sciences is a statewide flagship, public, academic health institution in Tennessee. Founded in 1911, the mission of the University of Tennessee Health Sciences is to improve the health and well-being of Tennesseans and the global community by fostering collaborative education, research, scientific discovery, clinical care, and public service. Employing more than 4,600 people on its faculty, staff, and not-for-profit corporation faculty practice groups, and with more than 3,200 students across the state, UT Health Sciences contributes $4 billion to the economy of Tennessee. Part of the University of Tennessee System, the Health Sciences is headquartered in Memphis and includes all six of UT’s doctoral-degree-granting health science colleges -- Medicine, Dentistry, Pharmacy, Nursing, Graduate Health Sciences, and Health Professions. UT Health Sciences spans the state with its four major, regional clinical health science locations in Memphis, Knoxville, Chattanooga, and Nashville, as well as more than 100 clinical education sites across Tennessee. UT Health Sciences is the largest educator of health care professionals in the state and operates the state’s largest residency and fellowship advanced training programs. Located in West Tennessee on the banks of the Mississippi River, Memphis is the second-largest city in the state and among the largest cities in the Southeast. The Greater Memphis metropolitan area has more than 1.3 million residents, and the city ranks among those with the lowest cost of living in the country. It is home to a vibrant restaurant scene, a revitalized Downtown, the Midtown Arts District, many scenic neighborhoods, and an actively growing medical district. Memphis boasts attractions, including Elvis Presley’s Graceland, the Memphis Grizzlies, historic Beale Street, the National Civil Rights Museum, the second-largest urban county park in the United States, and the Memphis in May World Championship Barbecue Cooking Contest. This position consist of clinical care, clinical supervision of trainees, departmental and university service, and teaching. There are options for protected research time for appropriate applicants. Active areas of research include prospective clinical and clinical trials in addiction, pain in aging, cancer, and dementia, large neuroimaging database analyses, clinical trials in child and adolescent psychiatry, epidemiology, outcomes, and service delivery research."
    },
    "https://fa-ewlq-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/9988": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor-Internal Medicine (Chattanooga)",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States The University of Tennessee Health Science Center, College of Medicine-Chattanooga, is seeking a full-time non-tenured Assistant Professor. The University of Tennessee Health Science Center, College of Medicine-Chattanooga, is seeking a full-time non-tenured Assistant Professor. The Geriatrics/Palliative Care Clerkship Director provides educational leadership and oversight of the Geriatrics/Palliative Care clerkship with UT Health Sciences College of Medicine. The Clerkship Director is responsible for ensuring a high-quality, well-coordinated clinical learning experience that meets College of Medicine curricular objectives and applicable accreditation standards. The Director works collaboratively with faculty, clinical preceptors, the Office of Undergraduate Medical Education, and relevant departmental and College leadership to support student learning, assessment, and continuing improvement of the clerkship. EDUCATION: MD, DO, or equivalent terminal clinical degree. LICENSES: Board-certification or eligibility in an appropriate clinical specialty. Active Tennessee Medical License in good standing. THE UNIVERSITY OF TENNESSEE HEALTH SCIENCES, COLLEGE OF MEDICINE - CHATTANOOGA Chattanooga’s metro area population is approximately 430,000, and the city was recently selected as Forbes Magazine’s Top 50 places to visit in the world! Surrounded by mountains, rivers and lakes, Chattanooga and Hamilton County, Tennessee, offer a little bit of everything – the fastest internet service in the western hemisphere, a thriving innovation business district and a booming downtown along the Tennessee River brimming with shops, dining and history. Awarded Outdoor Magazine’s “Best Town Ever” title for two years, the Scenic City always delivers. Provide overall leadership and oversight of the Geriatrics/Palliative Care clerkship, including coordination of clinical experiences and learning objectives. Ensure the clerkship curriculum aligns with UT Health Sciences College of Medicine educational objectives and required competencies. Recruit, orient, and collaborate with faculty and clinical preceptors to provide appropriate and consistent educational experiences. Monitor student clinical experiences and address concerns related to student learning, clinical sites, or the learning environment. Oversee student assessment and evaluation processes, ensuring timely, meaningful, and consistent feedback. Review clerkship evaluations and other outcome data to identify strengths, concerns, and opportunities for improvement. Lead ongoing curriculum review and quality improvement efforts in collaboration with the Office of Undergraduate Medical Education and relevant faculty. Communicate regularly with students, faculty, preceptors, clerkship staff, and UME leadership regarding clerkship expectations, performance, and operational needs. Participate in relevant UT Health Sciences of Medicine committees, faculty development, and educational initiatives as appropriate. Maintain appropriate documentation and ensure the clerkship meets applicable institutional and accreditation requirements. Complete other educational and administrative responsibilities related to the clerkship as assigned PATIENT CARE- 80%: Provide clinical care consistent with the faculty member’s specialty and clinical appointment, with responsibilities including direct patient care, participation in clinical services, and other clinical activities as assigned. RESEARCH/SCHOLARSHIP- 5%: Participate in scholarly activities appropriate to the faculty members’ interests and role, which may include clinical or educational research, quality improvement, presentations, publications, or other scholarly work. EDUCATION- 5%: Participate in medical student, resident, and/or interprofessional education through clinical teaching, didactic instruction, mentorship, and other educational activities consistent with the needs of the College of Medicine. SERVICE/LEADERSHIP- 10%: Serve as the Geriatrics/Palliative Care Clerkship Director, providing leadership and administrative oversight of the clerkship as outlined in the position description. Responsibilities include curriculum oversight, faculty and preceptor coordination, student assessment and evaluation, communication with students and faculty, and ongoing curriculum improvement."
    },
