    "https://fa-ewlq-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/9843": {
      "title": "Associate Professor and Director - Preston Medical Library/ Health Information Center (UT Health Sciences COM-Knoxville)",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Knoxville, TN, United States The Director of the Preston Medical Library/ Health Information Center reports to the Dean of the University of Tennessee Health Sciences College of Medicine, Knoxville (UT Health Sciences COM-Knoxville). The Library Director has the primary responsibility for providing vision and leadership for planning, directing, and evaluating services and resources. The library has a dual function, serving as both an academic medical center library and a patient-centered information resource. As such, it serves with equal emphasis the staff, faculty, students, and healthcare providers at the University of Tennessee Medical Center as well as its patients, family members, and community. This is a full-time non-tenure Associate Professor. The Director of the Preston Medical Library/ Health Information Center reports to the Dean of the University of Tennessee Health Sciences College of Medicine, Knoxville (UT Health Sciences COM-Knoxville). The Library Director has the primary responsibility for providing vision and leadership for planning, directing, and evaluating services and resources. The library has a dual function, serving as both an academic medical center library and a patient-centered information resource. As such, it serves with equal emphasis the staff, faculty, students, and healthcare providers at the University of Tennessee Medical Center as well as its patients, family members, and community. This is a full-time non-tenure Associate Professor. EDUCATION: Master's Degree in Library or Information Science from an ALA-accredited program is required. EXPERIENCE: Minimum five (5) years of experience in a health sciences library is required. Demonstrated leadership experience in health science libraries and/or professional organizations. LICENSES/CERTIFICATION: Medical Library Association (MLA), Academy of Health Information Professionals (AHIP) credentialing required within six (6) months of service. REQUIRED SKILLS AND ABILITIES: Ability to lead a team of professionals, promote team growth, and foster the development of team members. Demonstrated capacity to work collegially and effectively with diverse groups of administrative leaders, faculty, and professional staff of academic and healthcare institutions. Demonstration of successful planning, implementation and evaluation of library services and programs. Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Demonstrated service orientation and strong interpersonal skills. Demonstrated ability to foster local, regional, and national collaborations with other libraries and professionals. Demonstrated ability to develop and implement research projects and publish results. THE UNIVERSITY OF TENNESSEE HEALTH SCIENCES, COLLEGE OF MEDCINE - KNOXVILLE The University of Tennessee College of Medicine – Knoxville (UT Health Sciences COM-Knoxville) was initially established in 1956 as the UT Memorial Research Center and Hospital, which also provided the graduate medical and dental educational (GMDE) and research programs in Knoxville. The GMDE programs collectively became the UT Graduate School of Medicine with the formation of the University of Tennessee Medical Center (UT Medical) in 1991, and more recently were renamed the UT Health Sciences COM-Knoxville with the increased emphasis on undergraduate medical education in Knoxville as well as the statewide presence of UT Health Sciences. The mission of the Knoxville Campus is to advance medical education required to train the next generation of physicians and dentists, deliver comprehensive and compassionate care with our hospital partner, UT Medical, and conduct innovative research that is meaningful for patients and their families. Our college provides training for third- and fourth-year Medical Students, second-year Physician Assistant Students, as well as Graduate Medical Education for 11 primary residency programs and 17 subspecialty fellowships. UT Health Sciences COM-Knoxville consists of 548 faculty (258 paid), 245 residents and fellows and hosts an average of 40-50 medical students per month. UT Health Sciences COM-Knoxville also provides Continuing Medical Education services for faculty, residents and practicing physicians in our region, as well as Advanced Medical Simulation for students, residents, and faculty. The UT Health Sciences College of Medicine-Knoxville, in partnership with UT Medical, sponsors extensive translational research in both clinical and laboratory settings. The faculty collaborates with various institutions both regionally and nationally including other UT Health Sciences campuses, UTK, Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL), and other health science center institutions. Provides professional expertise, administrative leadership, and overall direction for the library, including strategic and financial planning. Leads all operational areas of the library, including allocation of financial resources and personnel. Develops and implements annual budget according to University of Tennessee policies. Manages the collection and analysis of data on all aspects of services and resources. Develops and distributes monthly reports to the UT Health Sciences COM-Knoxville leadership. Reports to University Health System administration as requested. Ensures library services and resources support the educational, research, healthcare, and service mission of the UT Medical Center campus. Leads innovation in providing services and resources. Supervises ongoing assessments of products and services, analyzing data to make informed decisions. Provides direct supervision to faculty librarians and library personnel. Participates in research and scholarly activities. Serves as liaison to Administration faculty and directors. Attends university and hospital committees, councils, and meetings as the library representative. Represents the library locally, regionally, and nationally."
    },
    "https://fa-ewlq-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/9842": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Division of Pediatric Surgery (College of Medicine- Knoxville)",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Knoxville, TN, United States THIS IS A NON-TENURE TRACK, PART-TIME 70% POSITION LOCATED IN KNOXVILLE, TN The University of Tennessee Health Sciences, College of Medicine- Knoxville is seeking a non-tenure track Assistant Professor in the Division of Pediatric Surgery. THIS IS A NON-TENURE TRACK, PART-TIME 70% POSITION LOCATED IN KNOXVILLE, TN The University of Tennessee Health Sciences, College of Medicine- Knoxville is seeking a non-tenure track Assistant Professor in the Division of Pediatric Surgery. EDUCATION: MD or DO from an accredited institution. Completion of Pediatric Surgery Residency. LICENSES: Board-certified or board-eligible by the American Board of Pediatric Surgery. Active Tennessee Medical license. THE TENNESSEE HEALTH SCIENCES, COLLEGE OF MEDICINE - KNOXVILLE The University of Tennessee College of Medicine – Knoxville (UT Health Sciences COM-Knoxville) was initially established in 1956 as the UT Memorial Research Center and Hospital, which also provided the graduate medical and dental educational (GMDE) and research programs in Knoxville. The GMDE programs collectively became the UT Graduate School of Medicine with the formation of the University of Tennessee Medical Center (UT Medical) in 1991, and more recently were renamed the UT Health Sciences COM-Knoxville with the increased emphasis on undergraduate medical education in Knoxville as well as the statewide presence of UT Health Sciences. The mission of the Knoxville Campus is to advance medical education required to train the next generation of physicians and dentists, deliver comprehensive and compassionate care with our hospital partner, UT Medical, and conduct innovative research that is meaningful for patients and their families. Our college provides training for third- and fourth-year Medical Students, second-year Physician Assistant Students, as well as Graduate Medical Education for 11 primary residency programs and 17 subspecialty fellowships. UT Health Sciences COM-Knoxville consists of 548 faculty (258 paid), 245 residents and fellows and hosts an average of 40-50 medical students per month. UT Health Sciences COM-Knoxville also provides Continuing Medical Education services for faculty, residents and practicing physicians in our region, as well as Advanced Medical Simulation for students, residents, and faculty. The UT Health Sciences College of Medicine-Knoxville, in partnership with UT Medical, sponsors extensive translational research in both clinical and laboratory settings. The faculty collaborates with various institutions both regionally and nationally including other UT Health Sciences campuses, UTK, Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL), and other health science center institutions. Responsible for the didactic education and surgical training of residents, medical students, and fellows."
    },
    "https://fa-ewlq-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/9829": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - College of Medicine-Chattanooga",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States The University of Tennessee Health Science Center, College of Medicine-Chattanooga, is seeking part-time non-tenured Assistant Professor. The University of Tennessee Health Science Center, College of Medicine-Chattanooga, is seeking part-time non-tenured Assistant Professor. EDUCATION: MD or DO. LICENSES: Board-certification. THE UNIVERSITY OF TENNESSEE HEALTH SCIENCES, COLLEGE OF MEDICINE - CHATTANOOGA Chattanooga’s metro area population is approximately 430,000, and the city was recently selected as Forbes Magazine’s Top 50 places to visit in the world! Surrounded by mountains, rivers and lakes, Chattanooga and Hamilton County, Tennessee, offer a little bit of everything – the fastest internet service in the western hemisphere, a thriving innovation business district and a booming downtown along the Tennessee River brimming with shops, dining, and history. Awarded Outdoor Magazine’s “Best Town Ever” title for two years, the Scenic City always delivers. Supervision and education of residents, fellows, and medical students."
    },
    "https://fa-ewlq-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/9449": {
      "title": "Clinical Coordinator and Instructor/Assistant Professor - College of Health Professions, Department of Diagnostic and Health Sciences",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States The University of Tennessee Health Sciences is seeking candidates to be the Clinical Coordinator for the Pathologists’ Assistant (Patha) Program, in the College of Health Professions, Department of Diagnostic and Health Sciences. The position is a 12-month, full-time, non-tenure-track position at the Instructor or Assistant Professor rank. The Clinical Coordinator’s responsibilities in the PathA program include managing the clinical rotation sites, maintaining affiliation agreements, writing and implementing the clinical rotation schedules, sending and maintaining the clinical handbook, distributing and collecting student and liaison clinical rotation evaluations, and recruiting new clinical rotation sites. This faculty member will participate in teaching, scholarship, and service/administration, as well as participate in the admissions process and serve as a member of the Progress and Promotion Committee for the PathA program. The University of Tennessee Health Sciences is seeking candidates to be the Clinical Coordinator for the Pathologists’ Assistant (Patha) Program, in the College of Health Professions, Department of Diagnostic and Health Sciences. The position is a 12-month, full-time, non-tenure-track position at the Instructor or Assistant Professor rank. The Clinical Coordinator’s responsibilities in the PathA program include managing the clinical rotation sites, maintaining affiliation agreements, writing and implementing the clinical rotation schedules, sending and maintaining the clinical handbook, distributing and collecting student and liaison clinical rotation evaluations, and recruiting new clinical rotation sites. This faculty member will participate in teaching, scholarship, and service/administration, as well as participate in the admissions process and serve as a member of the Progress and Promotion Committee for the PathA program. EDUCATION: Master’s Degree from a NAACLS-accredited Pathologists’ Assistant program. LICENSES/CERTIFICATION: Current ASCP certification as a Pathologist’s Assistant; or a PhD. in a related field of study with an ASCP certification. DEPARTMENTAL PREFERENCES: Applicants must have and demonstrate up-to-date clinical knowledge and experience in laboratory medicine both in theory and practice in Pathologists’ Assistants. Strong organizational skills, excellent interpersonal skills including communication, problem solving, student coaching, conflict management, and follow-through, are required. Previous didactic and clinical teaching experience is preferred. THE DEPARTMENT OF DIAGNOSTIC AND HEALTH SCIENCES The mission of the Department of Diagnostic and Health Sciences ( https://www.uthsc.edu/health-professions/diagnostic-health-sciences ) is to produce graduates who are competent, compassionate, who employ critical thinking skills, who are poised to become leaders in the fields of Cytopathology, Histopathology, Medical Laboratory Science, Health Informatics and Information Management, Pathologists’ Assistant and/or research. THE COLLEGE OF HEALTH PROFESSIONS The College of Health Professions ( https://www.uthsc.edu/health-professions/about/index.php ) serves as the administrative home for allied health programs offered through the Departments of Audiology and Speech Pathology, Department of Diagnostic and Health Sciences, Physical Therapy and Occupational Therapy. The College also houses a research arm, the Division of Rehabilitation Sciences ( https://www.uthsc.edu/health-professions/research/rehabilitation-sciences.php ) and includes the Muscle Biology and Sarcopenia Laboratory, the Muscle and Neural Laboratory, the Integrative Muscle Biology Laboratory, the Human Performance Clinical and Research Laboratory, and the Movement Science Laboratory. THE UNIVERSITY OF TENNESSEE HEALTH SCIENCES The University of Tennessee Health Sciences, Memphis campus is comprised of six colleges- Health Professions, Dentistry, Graduate Health Sciences, Pharmacy, Medicine, and Nursing. The Memphis Core Teaching Hospitals include: St. Jude Children’s Research Hospital, Methodist Healthcare, Regional One Health, Le Bonheur Children’s Hospital, Baptist Memorial Health Care, Memphis VA Medical Center, and St. Francis Hospital. UT Health Sciences has a long and rich tradition of accomplishments in basic, clinical, and translational research in a wide variety of disciplines focused on the health issues that impact our community, our region, the state of Tennessee, and the world. UT Health Sciences researchers are supported by both government grants and private funding. The Cancer Center Building opened in 2007 and is the Mid South’s only adult cancer research facility, housing 32 research laboratories and more than 65 scientists. The 140,000 square foot Translational Research Building opened in 2016 and currently houses 40 investigators focused on cardiovascular and related diseases. During the last fiscal year, UT Health Sciences generated approximately $85 million in sponsored programs, including NIH grants and grants from private foundations. For more information about UT Health Sciences research, go to ( https://uthsc.edu/research ). The Clinical Coordinator will develop and teach the following courses within the PathA program: Human Physiology for Pathologists’ Assistants (MSPA 515) course- primary Instructor. One of the Pathologists’ Assistant Seminar courses (MSPA 514 or 534)- primary Instructor. Pathologists Assistant Practicum I-II clinical rotation courses (MSPA 613, 622)- primary Instructor. Human Anatomy lab, Introduction to Surgical and Autopsy Pathology lab- secondary Instructor. Additional lectures and lab as assigned or desired. Clinical Coordinator will be expected to recruit students, participate in scholarly activities, professional service and other activities assigned by the Program Director and/or Department Chair."
    },
    "https://fa-ewlq-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/9787": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Department of Medicine, Infectious Disease",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States The University of Tennessee Health Sciences, Department of Medicine, Infectious Disease is seeking candidates for a full-time non-tenure Assistant Professor. The University of Tennessee Health Sciences, Department of Medicine, Infectious Disease is seeking candidates for a full-time non-tenure Assistant Professor. EDUCATION: M.D. Degree. LICENSE/CERTIFICATION: Eligible for Tennessee Licensure and Board Certified in Internal Medicine and Infectious Diseases. THE UNIVERSITY OF TENNESSEE HEALTH SCIENCES The University of Tennessee Health Sciences is a statewide flagship, public, academic health institution in Tennessee. Founded in 1911, the mission of the University of Tennessee Health Sciences is to improve the health and well-being of Tennesseans and the global community by fostering collaborative education, research, scientific discovery, clinical care, and public service. Employing more than 4,600 people on its faculty, staff, and not-for-profit corporation faculty practice groups, and with more than 3,200 students across the state, UT Health Sciences contributes $4 billion to the economy of Tennessee. Part of the University of Tennessee System, the Health Sciences is headquartered in Memphis and includes all six of UT’s doctoral-degree-granting health science colleges -- Medicine, Dentistry, Pharmacy, Nursing, Graduate Health Sciences, and Health Professions. UT Health Sciences spans the state with its four major, regional clinical health science locations in Memphis, Knoxville, Chattanooga, and Nashville, as well as more than 100 clinical education sites across Tennessee. UT Health Sciences is the largest educator of health care professionals in the state and operates the state’s largest residency and fellowship advanced training programs. Located in West Tennessee on the banks of the Mississippi River, Memphis is the second-largest city in the state and among the largest cities in the Southeast. The Greater Memphis metropolitan area has more than 1.3 million residents, and the city ranks among those with the lowest cost of living in the country. It is home to a vibrant restaurant scene, a revitalized Downtown, the Midtown Arts District, many scenic neighborhoods, an active medical district, and a burgeoning airport in the midst of a $214 million modernization. Memphis boasts attractions, including Elvis Presley’s Graceland, the Memphis Grizzlies, historic Beale Street, the National Civil Rights Museum, the second-largest urban county park in the United States, and the Memphis in May World Championship Barbecue Cooking Contest. This position involves 90% patient care in Regional One Health (UTROP Practice Plan), including inpatient and outpatient services. 10% UT effort for teaching and research."
    },
